problem:
There exists a regular hexagon $ABCDEF$ inscribed in a circle of radius $r$. Points $G$, $H$, $I$, $J$, $K$, and $L$ lie on the circle such that segments $AG$, $BH$, $CI$, $DJ$, $EK$, and $FL$ are each of length $r$, and these segments intersect the sides of the hexagon as shown below:

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, L;

r = 5;

A = dir(0);
B = dir(60);
C = dir(120);
D = dir(180);
E = dir(240);
F = dir(300);

G = dir(40);
H = dir(100);
I = dir(160);
J = dir(220);
K = dir(280);
L = dir(340);

draw(Circle((0,0),r));
draw(A--G);
draw(B--H);
draw(C--I);
draw(D--J);
draw(E--K);
draw(F--L);
draw(A--B--C--D--E--F--cycle);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, E);
label("$C$", C, W);
label("$D$", D, SW);
label("$E$", E, S);
label("$F$", F, SE);
label("$G$", G, NE);
label("$H$", H, N);
label("$I$", I, NW);
label("$J$", J, N);
label("$K$", K, E);
label("$L$", L, E);
[/asy]

If the area of hexagon $ABCDEF$ is $36\sqrt{3}$, find the area of the hexagon formed by the points $G$, $H$, $I$, $J$, $K$, and $L$.

What is the smallest positive integer that satisfies the following conditions:
- It leaves a remainder of 3 when divided by 5;
- It leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 7;
- It leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 9;
- It leaves a remainder of 0 when divided by 11?

Find all real numbers $x$ and $y$ that satisfy the following system of equations:
\begin{align*}
x + y &= \frac{20}{3}, \\
x^2 + y^2 &= \frac{200}{9}, \\
x^3 + y^3 &= \frac{2000}{27}.
\end{align*}

